407→                    
408→                           Erdős believed not. Erdős and Selfridge <a href="#cite-ErSe75-672" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('ErSe75','672'); return false;">[ErSe75]</a> proved that the product of consecutive integers is never a perfect power.<br><br>The theory of Pell equations implies that there are infinitely many pairs $n,d$ with $(n,d)=1$ such that $n(n+d)(n+2d)$ is a square.<br><br>Considering the question of whether the product of an arithmetic progression of length $k$ can be equal to an $\ell$th power:<ul>
409→<li>Euler proved this is impossible when $k=4$ and $\ell=2$,</li>
410→<li>Obláth <a href="#cite-Ob51-672" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Ob51','672'); return false;">[Ob51]</a> proved this is impossible when $(k,l)=(5,2),(3,3),(3,4),(3,5)$.</li>
411→<li>Marszalek <a href="#cite-Ma85-672" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('Ma85','672'); return false;">[Ma85]</a> proved that this is only possible for $k\ll_d 1$, where $d$ is the common difference of the arithmetic progression.</li>
412→<li>Györy, Hajdu, and Saradha <a href="#cite-GHS04-672" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('GHS04','672'); return false;">[GHS04]</a> proved this is impossible for $4\leq k\leq 5$.
413→</li><li>Bennett, Bruin, Györy, and Hajdu <a href="#cite-BBGH06-672" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('BBGH06','672'); return false;">[BBGH06]</a> proved this is impossible for $4\leq k\leq 11$, and also impossible for $k$ sufficiently large depending only on the number of prime divisors of $d$.</li>
414→<li> Györy, Hajdu, and Pintér <a href="#cite-GHP09-672" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('GHP09','672'); return false;">[GHP09]</a> have proved this is impossible for $4\leq k\leq 34$.</li>
415→<li> Bennett and Siksek <a href="#cite-BeSi20-672" onclick="event.preventDefault();addNewBox('BeSi20','672'); return false;">[BeSi20]</a> have proved that this is impossible for all $k$ sufficiently large and $\ell &gt; e^{10^k}$ prime.</li>
416→</ul>Jakob Führer has observed this is possible for integers in general, for example $(-6)\cdot(-1)\cdot 4\cdot 9=6^3$.
